    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures/errors).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily create-limit rule for `POST /api/owners`, rejecting with **429** once 100+ owners already exist for today (by `registrationDate`). Following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection pattern (mirroring the existing `RejectCityAtCapacity` chain):\n\n**New files:**\n- `RejectOverDailyLimit.java` \u2014 counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals today; throws `DailyLimitExceededException` at \u2265 100 (`DAILY_LIMIT`).\n- `DailyLimitExceededException.java` \u2014 checked exception (`extends Exception`).\n- `DailyLimitExceededExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` (429) with an `errors` body, matching the `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler` style.\n- `officefloor/escalation/\u2026DailyLimitExceededException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to the handler.\n\n**Wiring:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted a `rejectOverDailyLimit` step (under `transaction` governance) between `rejectCityAtCapacity` and `build`, so the check runs before the owner is built/saved.\n\nThe check counts only existing persisted owners against the limit (the 101st create fails), consistent with the acceptance test's \"create 100, then the 101st is rejected\" scenario, which now passes.",
